<TopicPage slug="subjects/journalism/intermediate/the-inverted-pyramid">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The inverted pyramid is <strong>the oldest information-architecture pattern in
    professional writing</strong> — hierarchy engineered for speed (the reader\'s and the
    editor\'s). Learning it is learning to decide what matters most in a story, which is the
    editor\'s core skill.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the shape, the lede, quotes and nut graf, the alternatives" widget="equationStep" config={pyramidCfg} />

  <h2>Writing craft, newsroom-grade</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Active voice, concrete nouns, short sentences:</strong> clarity is a moral position in journalism (the accessibility lesson\'s plain language, deadline-priced) — jargon excludes readers; plain verbs respect them.</li>
    <li><strong>Numbers need the same discipline:</strong> percentages with bases, comparisons with context ("up 20%" from what?) — the data lesson\'s honesty rules, copy-edited; a newsroom\'s worst mistakes are often arithmetic.</li>
    <li><strong>Headlines are a second craft:</strong> accurate, specific, honest to the story (the chart-title lesson\'s rule: state the finding) — the engagement lesson\'s clickbait temptation is a business-model argument, and headline integrity is the reader-facing edge of trust.</li>
    <li><strong>The cutline test:</strong> the wire story must survive being cut to any length — writing so the truth survives editing is a skill the pyramid trains and the essay never needs.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the AP Stylebook; Poynter\'s writing craft resources; the design lesson\'s progressive disclosure.</p>
</TopicPage>
